{"qid":"Q47261","names":["Duchy of Bavaria"],"title":"Duchy of Bavaria","endonym":{"native":"Herzogtum Bayern","lang":"de"},"capsule":"The Duchy of Bavaria was one of the stem duchies of medieval Germany, occupying the Danube basin east of the Lech River, originally settled by the Baiuvarii in the sixth century and brought under Carolingian control by Charlemagne who deposed Duke Tassilo III in 788. Through the medieval period it passed among the Ottonians, Welfs, and Wittelsbachs — who held it from 1180 until 1918 — making it one of the most durably dynastied territories in European history. The duchy was progressively elevated: Frederick I raised the Wittelsbach line in 1180, and in 1623 Maximilian I received the electoral dignity; it was transformed into the Kingdom of Bavaria in 1806 under Napoleon's reorganization.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Boundaries are documented at high resolution from Carolingian land grants onward; internal sub-divisions between the Palatinate and the duchy proper shifted frequently and require era-specific mapping.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Bavaria, historical duchy","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Bavaria"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Duchy of Bavaria","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Duchy_of_Bavaria"},{"label":"Britannica: Wittelsbach dynasty","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Wittelsbach-dynasty"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Duchy of Bavaria","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Louis II the Strict","nativeName":"Ludwig II. der Strenge","roman":"","start":1253,"end":1294,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null}